#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424327 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157299 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab96fd62-5043-501a-a3b5-5ee705f69d18" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral Over a Region Enclosed Between Two Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14619b02-c3bb-5be6-85af-30af1494c733" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424298} {:answer/id #uuid "441c7356-d182-50d5-a1bd-8b54901c3f5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424303} {:answer/id #uuid "7f18edf8-e5d2-5da6-9302-024ff289f750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424311} {:answer/id #uuid "91bb664e-dbb4-5152-b157-eaf96ebfee48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{26\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424313} {:answer/id #uuid "f40e93e6-995f-501f-964e-89e8ceda688e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{52\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f40e93e6-995f-501f-964e-89e8ceda688e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{52\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424321} :answer-field/id #uuid "64089c5c-af77-5ef9-b56b-0b6dff4fd9ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424323}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "45cc9fce-954c-547e-835b-7dec4ec6d636" :question/math-academy-id "q-161103" :question/problem "![](images/a5/a56bcec93b5cc66ef8238b7fe7b213c5e184f1cf4b11baf4524525a63a284780.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where the region $D$ is enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $3,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the annulus between the disks of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at the origin. \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ we obtain $r=1.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ we obtain $r=3.$\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}]}_{1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(9-\\frac{1}{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{52\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157834 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157298 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aad77980-4324-5d52-a93f-a37ab3390ef8" :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Integral Over a Region Enclosed Between Two Curves to Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63ff2307-bdea-53b3-88e5-97b96d5ad36a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489749} {:answer/id #uuid "6d4cd776-9de6-538f-9d94-7a3efcb04fad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489772} {:answer/id #uuid "6e4aa5b4-4bf8-52ba-bb0c-de13688b61cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489774} {:answer/id #uuid "c761b339-ab83-5bb8-80db-4cc0d6bebede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489946} {:answer/id #uuid "cce7b078-805e-5d09-a2f3-9ad87e8072c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cce7b078-805e-5d09-a2f3-9ad87e8072c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489969} :answer-field/id #uuid "d25f125e-94ad-5225-b5e5-f24049a7a3e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5d182d5c-4098-5732-b594-84e4e6e5bea2" :question/math-academy-id "q-83789" :question/problem "![](images/8b/8b694a0907a5cb1c33d44c921b01b8e351059ce3e695485d79b72051f7ed764a.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve ${x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1$ and the line $y=1,$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at $(0,1).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1 & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $y=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1 \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the points $(-1,1)$ and $(1,1).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/68/68f70968b0dd256159dbfdcbfde4f2f63578237db1770e019c8fb19a5a638eaa.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157839 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157298 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aad77980-4324-5d52-a93f-a37ab3390ef8" :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Integral Over a Region Enclosed Between Two Curves to Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18a268e5-f9a7-5175-82f6-429e900d7180" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394560} {:answer/id #uuid "7a8c7c83-2673-55cc-b80f-82dd032a52b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394561} {:answer/id #uuid "781c89a4-4fc2-5fdd-81c9-1fe0439559bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394562} {:answer/id #uuid "c04067e2-1051-59f7-ba78-7d685ba5c6ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{4}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394563} {:answer/id #uuid "7e63b9da-492f-5a21-ae92-0344b2806a9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394564}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18a268e5-f9a7-5175-82f6-429e900d7180" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394560} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e89d210-e453-5f03-84ad-9efe39e4cea9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394559}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "95f91c79-4c92-5753-8c02-6dfc6c0682b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-83980" :question/problem "![](images/92/928fb72015b78436f95895799c2846d1a0b34242b7216d31a6ed6bdda46728a1.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$ and the line $x=2\\sqrt{2},$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $4$ centered at $(0,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ we obtain $r=4.$\n\n- For $x=2\\sqrt{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(2\\sqrt{2},2\\sqrt{2}).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =0$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/37/375e270ce444d9f62b50f9a25abfae781113fdad895833d693061ffffe93f5d3.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}r\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157840 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157299 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab96fd62-5043-501a-a3b5-5ee705f69d18" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral Over a Region Enclosed Between Two Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "939507be-1c81-5ae7-8ee3-1c8b4987b74e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155962} {:answer/id #uuid "7c0de5db-9466-5fa3-9728-3739822adce3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155966} {:answer/id #uuid "339ef220-3e41-588b-8376-adbd3ff3fd12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489630} {:answer/id #uuid "68754c06-e278-58cb-aad9-4cc8f6c4502c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489765} {:answer/id #uuid "a1f50510-55ec-59ec-bc2f-bcefacaba339" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "339ef220-3e41-588b-8376-adbd3ff3fd12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489630} :answer-field/id #uuid "801f0ec1-f516-55ae-acb8-d095f0095e4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155961}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "932bfdfe-cb1f-5acf-bcb0-ab10a83a0361" :question/math-academy-id "q-83986" :question/problem "![](images/df/dfaa18cb3295d3bb1641f440a3c1642758fe793c3605cf7d010121a68bbff47d.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where the region $D$ in the first quadrant is enclosed between two quarter-circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $2,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion between the disks of radii $1$ and $2$ centered at the origin. \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ we obtain $r=1.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain $r=2.$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ The entire region lies between the radial lines $\\theta =0$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/46/4660d61851814c87afc7c140dd87e1354785f55cafe4fbc911fd369f768914e3.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{4-(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}r\\sqrt{4-{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{[-\\frac{1}{3}(4-{r}^{2})^{3/2}]}_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-\\frac{1}{3}(0-3\\sqrt{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}[\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424328 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157298 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aad77980-4324-5d52-a93f-a37ab3390ef8" :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Integral Over a Region Enclosed Between Two Curves to Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "055b0e6b-d664-5538-b782-3dfbe82f8ee7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi /3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424297} {:answer/id #uuid "320a2c42-e7e8-5a89-b8ed-772fad2d8845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi /3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424302} {:answer/id #uuid "4e20bf88-0220-51b3-8e06-d32a2b7d8220" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\frac{1}{r}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424305} {:answer/id #uuid "776ef258-4be4-56d1-84fa-73c7f0f2fd04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{1}{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424309} {:answer/id #uuid "cd6a190c-ce1c-504a-9a43-1fa603eb0266" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi /3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "055b0e6b-d664-5538-b782-3dfbe82f8ee7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi /3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424297} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc66dd31-0a57-5231-b848-a0871bc42f3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424326}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "78459587-308f-5867-9175-f728a41ccebc" :question/math-academy-id "q-85181" :question/problem "![](images/3d/3d5542933934102149c58153c3108d55d9c615e991db8aca8d8fc1e60837917f.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve ${x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4$ and the line $y=3,$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(0,2).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4 & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $y=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3 \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =3 \\\\ r & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(-\\sqrt{3},3).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/49/4903ca2ba8cde3f7874e10998a0741035ec7b2dea69a7804eaca64cb552fc572.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}}{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi /3}{\\int }_{3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\frac{{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi /3}{\\int }_{3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }r{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi /3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
